05b099c7-97e6-4478-b169-00e34e9477cf.body:
The process by which nodes of the Bitcoin network connect to other nodes in order to obtain information. When a Bitcoin node is launched for the first time, it has no information about the other nodes on the network. Yet it must establish connections in order to synchronize on the blockchain with the most accumulated work. Several mechanisms are used to discover these peers, in order of priority:
* The node starts by consulting its local `peers.dat` file, which stores information about the nodes it has previously interacted with. If the node is new, this file will be empty, and the process moves on to the next step;
* In the absence of information in the `peers.dat` file (which is normal for a newly launched node), the node makes DNS queries to the DNS seeds. These servers provide a list of IP addresses of nodes presumed to be active, to establish connections with. The addresses of the DNS seeds are hard-coded in the Bitcoin Core code. This step is generally enough to complete peer discovery;
* If the DNS seeds do not respond within 60 seconds, the node can then turn to the seed nodes. These are public Bitcoin nodes listed in a static list of nearly a thousand entries built directly into the Bitcoin Core source code. The new node will use this list to establish a first connection to the network and obtain IP addresses of other nodes;
* In the very unlikely case where all the previous methods fail, the node operator can always manually add node IP addresses to establish a first connection.

080efa61-7f74-4d60-9ee4-4fa10920b967.body:
In the context of hierarchical deterministic (HD) wallets, a derivation path refers to the sequence of indexes used to derive child keys from a master key. Described in BIP-0032, this path makes it possible to identify the tree structure used to derive child keys. A derivation path is represented by a series of indexes separated by slashes, and always starts with the master key (denoted `m/`). For example, a typical path could be `m/84'/0'/0'/0/0`. Each level of derivation is associated with a specific depth:
* `m /` indicates the master private key. It is unique to a wallet and cannot have siblings at the same depth. The master key is derived directly from the seed;
* `m / purpose' /` indicates the derivation purpose, which identifies the standard being followed. This field is described in BIP-0043. For example, if the wallet follows the BIP-0084 standard (SegWit V0), the index will then be `84'`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' /` indicates the type of cryptocurrency. This makes it possible to clearly separate the branches dedicated to one cryptocurrency from the branches dedicated to another cryptocurrency in a multi-coin wallet. The index dedicated to bitcoin is `0'`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' /` indicates the account number. This depth makes it easy to separate and organize a wallet into different accounts. These accounts are numbered starting from `0'`. Extended keys (`xpub`, `xprv`...) are found at this depth;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' / change /` indicates the chain. Each account as defined at depth 3 has two chains at depth 4: an external chain and an internal chain (also called "change"). The external chain derives addresses intended to be shared publicly, that is, the addresses that are offered to us when we click on "receive" in our wallet software. The internal chain derives addresses intended not to be shared publicly, that is, mainly change addresses. The external chain is identified with index `0` and the internal chain is identified with index `1`. You will notice that from this depth onward, a hardened derivation is no longer performed, but a normal derivation (there is no apostrophe). It is thanks to this mechanism that we are able to derive all child public keys from their `xpub`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' / change / address-index` simply indicates the number of the receiving address and of its key pair, in order to distinguish it from its siblings at the same depth on the same branch. For example, the first derived address has index `0`, the second address has index `1`, etc.

For example, if my receiving address has the derivation path `m / 86' / 0' / 0' / 0 / 5`, we can deduce the following information:
* `86'` indicates that we are following the derivation standard of BIP-0086 (Taproot / SegWit V1);
* `0'` indicates that it is a Bitcoin address;
* `0'` indicates that we are on the first account of the wallet;
* `0` indicates that it is an external address;
* `5` indicates that it is the sixth external address of this account.

![BIP-84 tree of an HD wallet, structured in six levels from the master key m down to the individual addresses.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/c/chemin-de-derivation/assets/image-1.png)

09891220-1ce7-4437-849c-1d3fd4f10f88.body:
A company specializing in Lightning payment solutions. Alby offers an ecosystem of complementary products:
* Alby Hub, a *self-custodial* Lightning node that can be self-hosted, deployed in the cloud, or used as an interface for a conventional node;
* the Alby browser extension, which makes it possible to make Lightning payments and to authenticate on websites directly from the browser;
* Alby Go, a mobile application to manage one's payments on the go.

Alby also provides APIs and the *Nostr Wallet Connect* (NWC) protocol to allow developers to integrate Lightning payments into third-party applications.

0ff5943f-fb59-4b9b-b650-ffca2a5e82fb.body:
Makes the transaction invalid unless all of these conditions are met:
* The stack is not empty;
* The value at the top of the stack is greater than or equal to `0`;
* The timelock type is the same between the `nLockTime` field and the value at the top of the stack (real time or block number);
* The input's `nSequence` field is not equal to `0xffffffff`;
* The value of the transaction's `nLockTime` field is greater than or equal to the value at the top of the stack.

If any one of these conditions is not met, the script containing the `OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY` cannot be satisfied. If all these conditions are valid, then `OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY` acts like a `OP_NOP`, that is, it performs no action on the script. It is a bit as if it disappeared. `OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY` therefore imposes a time constraint on spending the UTXO secured with the script containing it. It can do so either in the form of a date expressed in Unix time, or in the form of a block number. To do this, it restricts the possible values for the `nLockTime` field of the transaction that spends it, and this `nLockTime` field itself restricts the moment at which the transaction can be included in a block.

*This opcode is a replacement for `OP_NOP`. It was placed on the `OP_NOP2`. It is often referred to by its acronym “CLTV”. Note that `OP_CLTV` must not be confused with the `nLockTime` field of a transaction. The former uses the latter, but their natures and actions are different.*

121bd39a-dfa7-417a-99bf-f3bf120dc01b.body:
A framework providing a way to program scripts securely on Bitcoin. Bitcoin's native language is called script. It is quite complex to use in practice, notably for sophisticated and customized applications. Above all, it is very difficult to verify the limitations of a script. Miniscript uses a subset of Bitcoin scripts to simplify their creation, analysis and verification. Each miniscript is equivalent 1 for 1 to a native script. An easy-to-use *policies* language is used, which is then compiled into miniscript, to finally correspond to a native script. 

![Miniscript hierarchy: a policy compiles into miniscript, equivalent 1 to 1 to a native Bitcoin script.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/m/miniscript/assets/image-1.png)

Miniscript thus allows developers to build sophisticated scripts in a safer and more reliable way. The essential properties of Miniscript are as follows: 
* It allows static analysis of the script, notably of the spending conditions it allows and of its cost in terms of resources;
* It makes it possible to produce scripts that comply with consensus;
* It makes it possible to analyze whether or not the different spending paths comply with the standard rules of nodes;
* It makes it possible to establish a general, understandable and composable standard for all wallet software and hardware.

The Miniscript project was launched in 2018 by Pieter Wuille, Andrew Poelstra and Sanket Kanjalkar, through the company Blockstream. Miniscript was added to the Bitcoin Core wallet in *watch-only* mode in December 2022 with version 24.0, then fully in May 2023 with version 25.0.

12db32b8-fc26-4fb1-b8df-7bc172582b0d.body:
Acronym for *Distinguished Encoding Rules*. It is a strict subset of the ASN.1 encoding rules defined in the [ITU-T X.690, 2002](https://www.itu.int/ITU-T/studygroups/com17/languages/X.690-0207.pdf) specification and used to encode any type of data into a binary sequence. DER is mostly used in specific fields, such as cryptography, where data must be encoded in a standard and predictable way.

On Bitcoin, ECDSA signatures are encoded in DER format. They consist of two encoded numbers (`r`,`s`). The signature format consists of the following elements:

```text
0x30 | length |  0x02 | r-length | r | 0x02 | s-length | s
```

With:
* `0x30` (1 byte): identifier of a compound structure;
* `length` (1 byte): length of the following data;
* `0x02` (1 byte): data type identifier `INTEGER` (integer);
* `r-length` (1 byte): length of the following `r` value (generally 32 or 33 bytes);
* `r` (variable): `r` value as a big-endian integer (*big-endian*);
* `0x02` (1 byte): data type identifier `INTEGER` (integer);
* `s-length` (1 byte): length of the following `s` value (generally 32 or 33 bytes);
* `s` (variable): `s` value as a big-endian integer (*big-endian*).

In a Bitcoin transaction, a byte is added at the end of a DER signature to indicate the type of SigHash used.

131cc280-fddf-48bf-b1c6-3e4a1fefabc2.body:
In the RGB protocol, an *Anchor* represents a set of client-side data used to prove the inclusion of a unique commitment in a transaction. In the RGB protocol, an *Anchor* is made up of the following elements:
* The Bitcoin transaction identifier (TXID) of the *witness transaction*;
* The *Multi Protocol Commitment* (MPC) proof, comprising the depth, the cofactor, and the Merkle proof;
* The *Extra Transaction Proof* (ETP), which provides the additional data needed to validate a *Tapret*-type commitment (not required for *Opret*).

An *Anchor* therefore serves to establish a verifiable link between a specific Bitcoin transaction and private data validated by the RGB protocol. It guarantees that this data is indeed included in the blockchain, without its exact content being publicly exposed.

14740648-aa8c-4405-81fd-063c44928336.body:
A proof-of-work system designed by Adam Back in 1997 to fight spam and DoS attacks. It is based on the principle that a sender must perform computational work (specifically, searching for a partial collision on a cryptographic hash function) to prove their work. This task is costly in time and energy for the sender, but verification of the result by the recipient is quick and simple. This protocol proved particularly well suited to fighting spam in email systems, because it places little burden on legitimate users, while constituting a major obstacle for spammers. Indeed, sending a single email requires a few seconds of computation, but repeating this operation millions of times makes it extremely costly in terms of energy and time, which often cancels out the economic appeal of spam campaigns, whether for marketing or malicious purposes. Moreover, it preserves the anonymity of the sender. 

HashCash was quickly adopted by cypherpunks who were seeking to develop an anonymous electronic cash system without an intermediary. Indeed, Adam Back's innovation introduced for the first time the notion of scarcity in the digital world. The concept of proof of work is then found in several electronic money systems predating Bitcoin, including:
* b-money by Wei Dai, published in 1998;
* RPOW by Hal Finney, published in 2004;
* Bit Gold by Nick Szabo, published in 2005.

The principle of HashCash is also found within the Bitcoin protocol, where it is used as a protection mechanism against Sybil attacks.

1757fc8e-1179-4ec5-b7be-097d31ddb93f.body:
In hierarchical deterministic (HD) wallets, the purpose, defined by BIP-0043, represents a specific derivation level. This index, located at the first depth of the derivation tree (`m / purpose' /`), identifies the derivation standard adopted by the wallet, in order to facilitate compatibility between different wallet management software. For example, in the case of SegWit addresses (BIP-0084), the purpose is denoted `/84'/`. This method makes it possible to organize keys efficiently between the different address types within a single HD wallet. The standard indexes used are:
* For P2PKH: `44'`;
* For P2WPKH-nested-in-P2SH: `49'`;
* For P2WPKH: `84'`;
* For P2TR: `86'`.

![BIP-84 tree with an arrow pointing to the Purpose level (m/84'), which identifies the derivation standard followed.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/o/objectif/assets/image-1.png)

1bdb482e-52c8-48fe-b31c-d542311f0fc6.body:
Mathematical function that takes an input of variable size (called a message) and produces an output of fixed size (called a hash, digest, or fingerprint). Hash functions are widely used primitives in cryptography. They have specific properties that make them suitable for use in secure contexts:
* Preimage resistance: it must be very difficult to find a message that gives a specific hash, that is, to find a preimage `m` for a hash `h` such that `h = H(m)`, where `H` is the hash function;
* Second preimage resistance: given a message `m_1`, it must be very difficult to find another message `m_2` (different from `m_1`) such that `H(m_1) = H(m_2)`;
* Collision resistance: it must be very difficult to find two distinct messages `m_1` and `m_2` such that `H(m_1) = H(m_2)`;
* Tamper resistance: small changes in the input must cause significant and unpredictable changes in the output.

In the context of Bitcoin, hash functions are used for several purposes, notably for the proof-of-work mechanism (*Proof-of-Work*), transaction identifiers, address generation, checksum calculation, and the creation of data structures such as Merkle trees. On the protocol side, Bitcoin exclusively uses the `SHA256d` function, also called `HASH256`, which consists of a double hash `SHA256`. `HASH256` is also used in the calculation of certain checksums, notably for extended keys (`xpub`, `xprv`...). On the wallet side, the following are also used:
* Simple `SHA256` for the checksums of mnemonic phrases;
* `SHA512` within the `HMAC` and `PBKDF2` algorithms used in the derivation process of deterministic and hierarchical wallets;
* `HASH160`, which describes the successive use of a `SHA256` and a `RIPEMD160`. `HASH160` is used in the process of generating receiving addresses (except P2PK, P2WSH and P2TR) and in the calculation of parent key fingerprints for extended keys.

1c5cfca6-88b1-4560-a570-01fe897636a5.body:
In the context of RGB, a *Contract Participant* is an actor taking part in operations relating to the contract. A distinction is thus made between:
* The contract's *issuer*, who creates the *Genesis* (the origin of the contract);
* The *contract parties*, that is, the holders of rights over the contract state;
* The *public parties*, actors who can build *State Extensions* if the contract offers publicly accessible *Valencies*.

1e39c350-c6f2-43b3-a631-71169e38e229.body:
Refers to the digital signature operations required to validate transactions. Each Bitcoin transaction can contain several inputs, each of which may require one or more signatures to be considered valid. The verification of these signatures is done through the use of specific opcodes known as "sigops". Concretely, this includes `OP_CHECKSIG`, `OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY`, `OP_CHECKMULTISIG` and `OP_CHECKMULTISIGVERIFY`. These operations place a certain workload on the network nodes that must verify them. To prevent DoS attacks through artificial inflation of the number of sigops, the protocol therefore imposes a limit on the number of sigops allowed per block, in order to ensure that the validation workload remains manageable for nodes. This limit is currently 80,000 sigops maximum per block. To count them, nodes follow these rules:

In the `scriptPubKey`, `OP_CHECKSIG` and `OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY` count as 4 sigops. The opcodes `OP_CHECKMULTISIG` and `OP_CHECKMULTISIGVERIFY` count as 80 sigops. Indeed, during counting, these operations are multiplied by 4 when they are not part of a SegWit input (for a P2WPKH, the number of sigops will therefore be 1);

In the `redeemScript`, the opcodes `OP_CHECKSIG` and `OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY` are also worth 4 sigops, `OP_CHECKMULTISIG` and `OP_CHECKMULTISIGVERIFY` are counted as `4n` if they are preceded by `OP_n`, or 80 sigops otherwise;

For the `witnessScript`, `OP_CHECKSIG` and `OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY` are worth 1 sigop, `OP_CHECKMULTISIG` and `OP_CHECKMULTISIGVERIFY` are counted as `n` if they are introduced by `OP_n`, or 20 sigops otherwise;

In Taproot scripts, sigops are handled differently compared to traditional scripts. Instead of directly counting each signature operation, Taproot introduces a sigops budget for each transaction input, which is proportional to the size of that input. This budget is 50 sigops plus the size in bytes of the input's witness. Each signature operation reduces this budget by 50. If the execution of a signature operation causes the budget to fall below zero, the script is invalid. This method allows more flexibility in Taproot scripts, while maintaining protection against potential abuse related to sigops, by tying them directly to the weight of the input. Thus, Taproot scripts are not taken into account in the limit of 80,000 sigops per block.

1f0d5de7-0f3b-4fd3-84f0-6e7c24ac0509.body:
The term "fork" has several meanings in the context of Bitcoin. It refers either to a split of the network of nodes into several separate groups, leading to the creation of several different blockchains, or to a change in the protocol rules, or even to both simultaneously. To simplify, we distinguish 4 main categories of forks:
* The natural fork: occurs when there is temporary competition between two blocks discovered at the same time at the same height. This fork can extend over several blocks. This type of fork resolves naturally when one of the chains becomes longer than the other (with more accumulated work), leading to a reorganization. This reorganization manifests itself with all the nodes agreeing again on a single blockchain;
* The code fork: consists of creating an entirely new cryptocurrency from Bitcoin's source code, by launching a new blockchain from the Genesis block;
* The hard fork: represents a change to the Bitcoin protocol, incompatible with previous versions, by removing rules or by loosening existing ones. It results in the creation of two distinct and incompatible chains if not all nodes are updated. The network then splits in two: those who adopt the new rules and those who keep the old ones;
* The soft fork: involves backward-compatible changes that add rules or make existing ones more restrictive, without causing a network split. Nodes that do not adopt the new rules can continue to follow the same chain as the others, provided that the majority of the network's computing power supports the updated chain.

1fcc0d0b-d8b1-4f13-a3e3-f67383f704d4.body:
Document that defines the process for developing and implementing improvements proposed to the Bitcoin protocol. Written by Amir Taaki in September 2011, it establishes a standard methodology for proposing and documenting potential changes. BIP proposals are then classified into three categories: 
* `Standards Track`: those that concern direct modifications of the Bitcoin protocol and its interoperability;
* `Informational`: those that offer general information, but do not directly affect the protocol; 
* `Process`: those that introduce non-technical changes, such as procedures and guidelines.

This framework systematizes the development of Bitcoin, in order to ensure a coordinated and transparent approach to its evolution. The BIP-0001 methodology would later be replaced by that of BIP-0002.

204df0cf-f8e9-49a5-817a-3380953664d1.body:
In the context of Samourai Wallet and other wallet software that implements it, a Cahoot refers to all types of transactions carried out collaboratively between several users. Carrying out a Cahoot therefore means jointly taking part in a transaction. This collaboration is built around the exchange of partially signed transactions. These exchanges can be done either manually, via QR codes, or automatically, via the *Soroban* communication network. Cahoots include:
* *Stowaway* transactions (*Payjoin*);
* *Stonewall x2* transactions;
* *Joinbot* transactions.

29193a69-fe44-4bf3-9236-5d427db370fd.body:
Introduces the bech32 address format for SegWit V0 addresses. This address format is characterized by the prefix `bc1q`. The bech32 format offers several advantages: 
* It requires less space in QR codes;
* It is more easily interpretable by humans;
* It has an innovative checksum mechanism that performs better and makes it possible to detect and potentially automatically correct typing errors.
These characteristics make receiving addresses easier to use while minimizing the risk of errors.

2c81d29e-66ab-4a84-8ecc-69805aa39e85.body:
A specific element of a Bitcoin transaction that specifies the source of the funds used to satisfy the outputs. Each `vin` refers to an unspent output (UTXO) of a previous transaction. A transaction can contain several inputs, each one identified by a combination of the `txid` (the identifier of the originating transaction) and the `vout` (the index of the output in that transaction).

Each `vin` includes the following information:
* `txid`: the identifier of the previous transaction containing the output used here as an input;
* `vout`: the index of the output in the previous transaction;
* `scriptSig` or `scriptWitness`: an unlocking script that provides the data needed to satisfy the conditions set by the `scriptPubKey` of the previous transaction whose funds are being spent, generally by providing a cryptographic signature;
* `nSequence`: a specific field used to indicate how this input is timelocked, as well as other options such as RBF.

3b1dd060-9cee-4c26-8c25-5d8150cf77ab.body:
A string of characters that combines a key (public or private), its associated chain code and a series of metadata. An extended key gathers into a single identifier all the elements necessary for deriving child keys. They are used in hierarchical deterministic wallets, and can be of two types: an extended public key (used to derive child public keys) or an extended private key (used to derive both child private keys and child public keys). An extended key therefore includes several different pieces of data, described in BIP-0032, in the following order:
* The prefix: `prv` and `pub` are HRPs indicating whether we are dealing with an extended private key (`prv`) or an extended public key (`pub`). The first letter of the prefix designates the version of the extended key: `x` for Legacy or SegWit V1 on Bitcoin, `t` for Legacy or SegWit V1 on Bitcoin Testnet, `y` for Nested SegWit on Bitcoin, `u` for Nested SegWit on Bitcoin Testnet, `z` for SegWit V0 on Bitcoin, `v` for SegWit V0 on Bitcoin Testnet.
* The depth, which indicates the number of derivations that took place from the master key to arrive at the extended key;
* The parent fingerprint. This represents the first 4 bytes of the `HASH160` of the parent public key;
* The index. This is the number of the pair, among its siblings, from which the extended key is derived;
* The chain code;
* A padding byte if it is a private key `0x00`;
* The private key or the public key;
* A checksum. It represents the first 4 bytes of the `HASH256` of all the rest of the extended key.

In practice, the extended public key is used to generate receiving addresses and to observe the transactions of an account, without exposing the associated private keys. This can allow, for example, the creation of a so-called "watch-only" wallet. It is however important to note that the extended public key is sensitive information for the user's privacy, because its disclosure can allow third parties to trace the transactions and see the balance of the associated account.

44552511-eb19-4d7a-a3bb-c9d13b3fee50.body:
A constructed and signed Bitcoin transaction, which is in its binary form. A raw transaction (*raw TX*) is the final representation of a transaction, just before it is broadcast on the network. This transaction contains all the information necessary for its inclusion in a block:
* The version;
* The flag;
* The inputs;
* The outputs;
* The locktime;
* The witness.

What is called a "*raw transaction*" represents the raw data that is passed twice through the `SHA256` hash function to generate the transaction's TXID. For SegWit transactions, only the original serialization (without the *marker*, the *flag* and the *witness*) is used to calculate the TXID. This data is then used in the block's Merkle tree to integrate the transaction into the blockchain.

*This concept is also sometimes called "Serialized Transaction".*

46d9de8a-69c5-4b21-99d6-e88ae46e3b02.body:
A high-performance, high-security C library for digital signatures and other cryptographic primitives on the elliptic curve `secp256k1`. Since this curve has never been widely used outside of Bitcoin (unlike the curve `secp256r1` that is often preferred), this library aims to be the most complete reference for its use. The development of libsecp256k1 has been primarily oriented toward Bitcoin's needs, and features intended for other applications may be less tested or verified. Proper use of this library requires particular care, in order to make sure it suits the specific objectives of applications other than Bitcoin.

The libsecp256k1 library offers a variety of features, including:
* ECDSA-secp256k1 signing and verification, as well as cryptographic key generation;
* Additive and multiplicative operations on secret and public keys;
* Serialization and parsing of secret keys, public keys and signatures;
* Constant-time, constant-memory-access signing and public key generation;
* And a multitude of other cryptographic primitives.

501e3e0a-3d9d-447b-82be-991828b5f05e.body:
Regulatory procedure used by certain businesses operating in Bitcoin to verify the identity of their customers as part of the fight against money laundering and the financing of terrorism. KYC involves the collection and verification of personal data. In the context of buying bitcoins, KYC creates several risks for the user, notably:
* The risk of personal data leaks tied to Bitcoin activity. Storing information on company servers can lead to leaks, exposing users' data to phishing attempts, physical attacks, or identity theft, particularly because of its association with the Bitcoin environment;
* Exposure to state surveillance. Buying BTC through regulated actors can reveal to the state that the user owned bitcoin at some point, which could have future consequences in the event of political or economic upheaval;
* Facilitation of on-chain tracing. Completing KYC creates a direct link between the user's identity and their transactions on the blockchain, making it possible to establish an entry point for chain analysis.

58295725-9bd8-4bb3-84ff-db983061c164.body:
Developed following the debates on SegWit, which used BIP-0009 for its activation, BIP-0008 is a soft fork activation method that natively incorporates an automatic UASF (*User-Activated Soft Fork*) mechanism. Like BIP-0009, BIP-0008 uses miner signaling, but adds the parameter `LOT` (*Lock-in On Time out*). If `LOT` is set to `vrai`, when the signaling period expires without reaching the required threshold, a UASF is automatically triggered, forcing the activation of the soft fork. This approach forces miners to be cooperative or risk a UASF imposed by users. Moreover, unlike BIP-0009, BIP-0008 defines the signaling period based on block height, eliminating potential manipulation by miners via hash rate. BIP-0008 also allows a variable voting threshold to be set and introduces a parameter for a minimum block height for activation, giving miners time to prepare and signal their agreement in advance without necessarily being ready. When a soft fork is activated via BIP-0008 with the parameter `LOT=vrai`, a very aggressive method is used against miners, who are immediately put under the pressure of a possible UASF. Indeed, this leaves them with only 2 choices:
* Be cooperative, and thus facilitate the activation process;
* Be uncooperative, in which case users automatically perform a UASF to impose the soft fork.

58ea6784-71a5-4c24-a2b1-0ace80de605b.body:
Bitcoin is the name of the peer-to-peer electronic cash system created by Satoshi Nakamoto in 2009. Using the term Bitcoin with a capital "B" can refer to three different things:
* The Bitcoin system;
* The Bitcoin protocol;
* The Bitcoin network.

The term bitcoin with a lowercase "b" is generally reserved for the monetary unit exchanged on this system.

![Bitcoin emblem with the capital B, used to refer to the system, the protocol or the network.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/b/bitcoin-b-majuscule/assets/image-1.png)

639ab934-b26f-4520-b282-5f310ec93859.body:
Refers to a block with no child: a valid block, but one excluded from the main Bitcoin chain. It occurs when two miners find a valid block at the same chain height within a short period of time and broadcast it on the network. Nodes eventually choose a single block to include in the chain, according to the principle of the chain with the most accumulated work, rendering the other “stale”. The process leading to the production of a stale block is as follows:
* Two miners find a valid block at the same chain height within a short time interval. Let's call them `Bloc A` and `Bloc B`;
* Each broadcasts its block to the network of Bitcoin nodes. Each node now has 2 blocks at the same height. There are therefore two valid chains;
* The miners continue searching for proofs of work for the next blocks, but to do so, they must choose a single block, between `Bloc A` and `Bloc B`, on top of which they will mine;
* A miner finally finds a valid block on top of `Bloc B`. Let's call it `Bloc B+1`;
* It broadcasts `Bloc B+1` to the nodes of the network;
* Since nodes follow the longest chain (the one with the most accumulated work), they will consider that `Chaîne B` is the one to follow;
* They will abandon `Bloc A`, which is no longer part of the main chain. It has therefore become a stale block.

![Temporary fork at height 145: block 145-A becomes stale while 145-B is extended by block 146.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/o/obsolete-bloc/assets/image-1.png)

*In French, one can also say “bloc périmé” or “bloc abandonné”. Even though I do not agree with this usage, some bitcoiners use the term “orphan block” to refer to what is in reality a stale block.*

643a18d6-6e51-4812-8aaa-e0c6e35f421b.body:
Initial connection points for new Bitcoin nodes joining the network. These seeds, which are in fact specific DNS servers, have their address permanently built into the Bitcoin Core code. When a new node starts, it contacts these servers to obtain a random list of IP addresses of Bitcoin nodes that are presumably active. The new node can thus establish connections with the nodes on this list in order to obtain the information needed to perform its IBD and synchronize on the chain with the most accumulated work. In 2024, here is the list of Bitcoin Core's DNS seeds and the people responsible for maintaining them (https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/master/src/kernel/chainparams.cpp):
* seed.bitcoin.sipa.be: Pieter Wuille;
* dnsseed.bluematt.me: Matt Corallo;
* dnsseed.bitcoin.dashjr-list-of-p2p-nodes.us: Luke Dashjr;
* seed.bitcoin.jonasschnelli.ch: Jonas Schnelli;
* seed.btc.petertodd.net: Peter Todd;
* seed.bitcoin.sprovoost.nl: Sjors Provoost;
* dnsseed.emzy.de: Stephan Oeste;
* seed.bitcoin.wiz.biz: Jason Maurice;
* seed.mainnet.achownodes.xyz: Ava Chow.

DNS seeds are the second means, in order of priority, for a Bitcoin node to establish connections. The first means is to use the `peers.dat` file that the node itself created. This file is naturally empty in the case of a new node, unless the user has modified it manually.

*Note that DNS seeds must not be confused with “seed nodes”, which are the third way of establishing connections.*

666c23e2-51a8-473d-b244-e6c5f37aef4d.body:
Variable-length unsigned integer encoding format used in the Bitcoin protocol to indicate the number of elements or the size of certain fields in transactions and network messages. CompactSize saves space by using fewer bytes for small values. The encoding works as follows:
* values from 0 to 252 are encoded on a single byte;
* values from 253 to 65,535 are preceded by the byte `0xfd` and encoded on 2 bytes in *little-endian*;
* values from 65,536 to 4,294,967,295 are preceded by `0xfe` and encoded on 4 bytes;
* larger values are preceded by `0xff` and encoded on 8 bytes.

CompactSize is used, for example, to indicate the number of transactions in a block, the number of inputs or outputs in a transaction, or the length of a script.

6bdf624c-36a7-48a0-8a63-122ec3d65e08.body:
Acronym for “*Partially Signed Bitcoin Transaction*”. It is a specification introduced with BIP-0174 to standardize the way unfinalized transactions are constructed in Bitcoin-related software, such as wallet software. A PSBT encapsulates a transaction in which the inputs may not be fully signed. It includes all the information necessary for a participant to be able to sign the transaction without requiring additional data. Thus, a PSBT can take 3 different forms:
* A fully constructed transaction, but not yet signed;
* A partially signed transaction, where some inputs are signed while others are not yet;
* Or a fully signed Bitcoin transaction, which can be converted to be ready for broadcast on the network.

The PSBT format facilitates interoperability between different wallet software and signing devices (hardware wallets). Currently, version 0 of PSBTs is used, as defined in BIP-0174, and version 2 was defined in BIP-0370.

*PSBT version 1 does not exist. Since version 0 was the first version, the second version was colloquially called version 2. Ava Chow, who wrote BIP-0370, therefore decided to give the number 2 to this new version in order to avoid any confusion.*

6d6f32a0-761e-4ee4-a189-db65aebc23ef.body:
Acronym for “*Hashed Timelock Contract*”. It is a smart contract mechanism mainly used on Lightning. It is also sometimes found in *atomic swaps*. Fundamentally, the HTLC makes it possible to make a transfer of funds conditional on the revelation of a secret, and also includes a time condition to protect the sender's money in case the transaction fails.

On Lightning, HTLCs make it possible to send bitcoins to a node with which one does not have a direct channel, by passing through several channels, without needing trust along the route. The payment between each node is conditional on the provision of a preimage (the secret which, when hashed, matches an agreed value). If the final recipient provides this preimage, they can claim the funds, and necessarily allow each intermediate node to also claim the funds in cascade. 

For example, if Alice wants to send 1 BTC to David, but she does not have a direct channel with him, she must go through Bob and Carol, who have payment channels between them. Here is how the transaction unfolds:
* David presents a Lightning *invoice* to Alice. It contains the hash `h` of a secret `s` (the preimage) that only David knows. We therefore have: `h\ =\ hachage(s)`;
* Alice creates an HTLC with Bob, offering to send him 1 BTC on condition that Bob provides her with a secret `s` (the preimage) that matches the hash `h`;
* Bob creates an HTLC with Carol, offering to send her 1 BTC on condition that she provides the same secret `s`;
* Carol creates an HTLC with David, offering 1 BTC again if he reveals the preimage `s`;
* David reveals `s` (which he was the only one to know) to Carol to receive 1 BTC. Carol can now use `s` to recover the BTC from Bob. And Bob, who now knows `s`, does the same with Alice.

Ultimately, Alice sent 1 BTC to David via Bob and Carol (a neutral action for the latter), without anyone having to trust each other, because everything is secured in cascade by the conditions of the HTLCs.

HTLCs thus make it possible to carry out so-called “atomic” exchanges: either the transfer is entirely successful, or it fails, and the funds are returned. This guarantees the security of transactions even between several participants without needing trust.

71ea0f44-ee11-407e-b024-1292775a3cdd.body:
A mechanism that makes it possible to establish a connection between Bitcoin's main system and a sidechain (or a drivechain), that is, a side chain. The two-way peg ensures a fixed value correlation between the bitcoins on the main blockchain and the corresponding assets on the sidechain, thus making it possible to move bitcoins between the two chains. To do this, the bitcoins are temporarily locked on the main blockchain and an equivalent amount of assets is issued on the sidechain. This makes it possible to benefit from the specific advantages of the sidechain, such as faster transactions or improved privacy features, while maintaining the value of the bitcoins used. When users wish to return to the Bitcoin blockchain, the process is reversed: the assets on the sidechain are destroyed and the corresponding bitcoins are unlocked. There are many different two-way peg mechanisms, which can rely on:
* A single trusted third party;
* A federation of entities;
* The miners of the main chain (drivechain).

89f252be-530d-4502-ac02-552058abdc1c.body:
Makes the transaction invalid if any one of these characteristics is observed:
* The stack is empty;
* The value at the top of the stack is less than `0`;
* The disable flag of the value at the top of the stack is not set and; The transaction version is less than `2` or; The disable flag of the input's `nSequence` field is set or; The timelock type is not the same between the `nSequence` field and the value at the top of the stack (real time or number of blocks) or; The value at the top of the stack is greater than the value of the input's `nSequence` field.

If one or more of these characteristics is observed, the script containing the `OP_CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY` cannot be satisfied. If all the conditions are valid, then `OP_CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY` acts like a `OP_NOP`, that is, it performs no action on the script. It is a bit as if it disappeared. `OP_CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY` therefore imposes a relative time constraint on spending the UTXO secured with the script containing it. It can do so either in the form of a real time, or in the form of a number of blocks. To do this, it restricts the possible values for the `nSequence` field of the input that spends it, and this `nSequence` field itself restricts the moment at which the transaction that includes this input can be included in a block.

*This opcode is a replacement for `OP_NOP`. It was placed on the `OP_NOP3`. It is often referred to by its acronym “CSV”. Note that `OP_CSV` must not be confused with the `nSequence` field of a transaction. The former uses the latter, but their natures and actions are different.*

8bb02eb3-92a6-4720-8b52-782bfe0517f2.body:
The term "halving" refers to a scheduled event that cuts in half the reward granted to miners for each block mined via the block subsidy. This reduction applies specifically to the part of the block subsidy made up of new bitcoins created ex nihilo. The halving was designed by Satoshi Nakamoto, the creator of Bitcoin, as a mechanism to control inflation and ensure a limited supply of bitcoins.

The initial block subsidy was 50 bitcoins, and the halving occurs every 210,000 blocks mined, which takes approximately four years. The halvings have taken place:
* At block 210,000 on November 28, 2012, reducing the subsidy to 25 BTC;
* At block 420,000 on July 9, 2016, reducing the subsidy to 12.5 BTC;
* At block 630,000 on May 11, 2020, reducing the subsidy to 6.25 BTC;
* At block 840,000 on April 20, 2024, reducing the subsidy to 3.125 BTC.

Halvings will continue to occur until the block subsidy reaches zero, at which point the maximum supply of approximately 21 million bitcoins will have been reached. The next Bitcoin halving is expected to take place around spring 2028, although the exact date may vary slightly depending on block mining time. At that point, the block subsidy will be reduced from 3.125 to 1.5625 bitcoins. This will be the fifth halving in Bitcoin's history. As block subsidies decrease, transaction fees become an increasingly important source of revenue for miners, which ensures their motivation to continue participating in proof of work.

8e8c8156-ba4a-44c5-8f56-ffe6f72f28fd.body:
In BIP-0047, a reusable payment code is a static identifier generated from a Bitcoin wallet that makes it possible to initiate a notification transaction and to derive unique addresses. This avoids address reuse, which leads to a loss of privacy, without having to manually derive and transmit new fresh addresses for each payment. In BIP-0047, reusable payment codes are constructed as follows:
* Byte 0 corresponds to the version;
* Byte 1 is a bit field making it possible to add information in case of specific use;
* Byte 2 indicates the parity of the `y` of the public key;
* From byte 3 to byte 34, you will find the `x` value of the public key;
* From byte 35 to byte 66, there is the chain code associated with the public key;
* From byte 67 to byte 79, there is zero padding.

A version byte (`0x47`) is generally added at the start of the payment code and a checksum at the end, then it is encoded in Base58Check. The construction of a payment code is therefore quite similar to that of an extended key. Here is my old BIP-0047 payment code in Base58Check, for example:

```text
PM8TJSBiQmNQDwTogMAbyqJe2PE2kQXjtgh88MRTxsrnHC8zpEtJ8j7Aj628oUFk8X6P5rJ7P5qDudE4Hwq9JXSRzGcZJbdJAjM9oVQ1UKU5j2nr7VR5
```

In the PayNym implementation of BIP-0047, payment codes can also be expressed in the form of identifiers associated with a robot image. Here is my old one, for example: 

```text
+throbbingpond8B1
```

The use of payment codes with the PayNym implementation is currently available on Sparrow Wallet on PC and on Ashigaru on mobile (a fork of Samourai Wallet, whose servers were seized by US authorities in April 2024).

927b5e3d-e290-41e1-b6c0-ec86da69de6f.body:
In the context of the RGB protocol, refers to an update of the contract state carried out according to the rules of the Schema. The following operations exist in RGB:
* State Transition;
* Genesis;
* State Extension.

Each operation modifies the state by adding or replacing certain data (Global State, Owned State…).

a63da214-1470-4dd7-aa04-8009088924d1.body:
*Contract Rights* refer to the different rights that the actors of an RGB contract can exercise. They are classified into several categories:
* The *ownership rights*, associated with holding a particular UTXO (via a Seal Definition);
* The *executive rights*, that is, the ability to build one or more transitions (State Transitions) compliant with the Schema;
* The *public rights*, when the Schema authorizes certain public uses, for example the creation of a State Extension via the redemption of a Valency.

ac5be5f7-85b3-4dba-b006-14b9bf55a40c.body:
Acronym for “*Bitcoin Improvement Proposal*”. A BIP is a formal document proposing an improvement, a technical specification or information relating to the Bitcoin protocol and its ecosystem. Since Bitcoin has no central entity to decide on updates, BIPs allow the community to suggest, discuss and implement improvements in a structured and transparent way.

Each BIP details the objectives of the proposed improvement, the rationale, the potential impacts on compatibility, as well as the advantages and drawbacks. BIPs can be written by any member of the community. They are primarily owned by their authors and represent their personal recommendation: an individual BIP does not constitute community consensus or a general implementation recommendation.

The editors who maintain the BIPs GitHub repository are: Bryan Bishop, Jon Atack, Luke Dashjr, Mark Erhardt (Murch), Olaoluwa Osuntokun and Ruben Somsen. Their role is administrative and editorial: they check the formatting, relevance and quality of proposals, but do not assess whether a proposal will be adopted. If someone proposes an improvement that is not accepted within the formal BIP framework, they can still present it directly to the Bitcoin community, or even create a fork including their modification. The advantage of the BIP process lies in its formality and centralization, which facilitate debate in order to avoid division among Bitcoin users, by seeking to implement updates by consensus. In the end, it is indeed the principle of economic majority that determines the power dynamics within the protocol.

BIPs are classified into three types (since BIP-0003, which replaced BIP-0002 in 2025):
* `Specification BIPs` (formerly “Standards Track”): Define a set of technical rules describing a new feature or affecting the interoperability of implementations. They can be implemented, and implementations can conform to their specification. They must contain a reference implementation and test vectors before reaching “Complete” status;
* `Informational BIPs`: Describe a design issue, provide general recommendations or information useful to the Bitcoin community;
* `Process BIPs`: Describe a process surrounding Bitcoin or propose a change to a process. They are treated as living documents, modifiable indefinitely by rough consensus on the mailing list.

“Specification” and “Informational” BIPs can be classified by layer (“Layer”):
* `Consensus (soft fork)` and `Consensus (hard fork)`: Concern Bitcoin's consensus rules, such as modifications to block or transaction validation rules. For example, the SegWit and Taproot BIPs belong to this category;
* `Peer Services`: Concerns the operation of the Bitcoin node network, that is, how nodes find and communicate with each other over the Internet;
* `API/RPC`: Concerns the application programming interfaces (APIs) and remote procedure calls (RPCs) that allow Bitcoin software to interact with nodes;
* `Applications`: Concerns applications built on top of Bitcoin. BIPs in this category typically address changes at the level of Bitcoin wallet standards.

The BIP submission process begins with conceptualizing and discussing the idea on the *Bitcoin Development Mailing List*. Authors must first verify that the idea has not already been proposed, then present it publicly to gather initial feedback. If the idea is promising and has generated sufficient interest in the community, the author writes a BIP following a specific format (MediaWiki or Markdown) and submits it via a Pull Request on the BIPs GitHub repository. The editors verify that the proposal complies with formatting, is relevant, has been previously discussed on the mailing list, and is technically feasible. A number is assigned by an editor only after the proposal has generated tangible interest in the community. Authors can also appoint “Deputies”, co-owners of the BIP who assist them in the process.

BIPs go through four statuses during their lifecycle (simplified by BIP-0003 from the nine statuses of BIP-0002):
* Draft (`Draft`): The proposal is being written and discussed. A BIP in “Draft” with no activity for one year may be closed if its authors do not show intent to continue;
* Complete (`Complete`): The authors have finished the intended work, the proposal is clear, complete and ready for adoption. “Specification” BIPs must include a reference implementation and test vectors. After this status, changes must remain minor. This is the final status of most successful informational BIPs;
* Deployed (`Deployed`): The BIP is actively used in the ecosystem. This status is reached upon presentation of evidence of actual use (production deployment by an established project, soft fork activation criteria met, etc.). Once deployed, the BIP is considered final and should no longer undergo substantial modifications;
* Closed (`Closed`): The BIP is no longer actively developed or used and is of historical interest only. This status replaces the former statuses “Deferred”, “Rejected”, “Withdrawn”, “Replaced” and “Obsolete”.

After reaching “Complete” status, any change must be documented in a “Changelog” section and versioned according to a semantic scheme.

b8d328dd-a9bc-4aaf-b87e-32f736585c20.body:
A practice that brings together all the methods for tracing bitcoin flows on the blockchain. In general, chain analysis relies on observing characteristics in samples of earlier transactions. It then consists of spotting these same characteristics in a transaction one wishes to analyze, and drawing plausible interpretations from them. This problem-solving method, based on a practical approach to find a good enough solution, is what is called a heuristic. To put it simply, chain analysis is done in two main steps: 
* Spotting known characteristics;
* Deducing hypotheses.

One of the goals of chain analysis is to group various activities on Bitcoin in order to determine whether they were carried out by a single user. Afterwards, it will be possible to try to link this bundle of activities to a real-world identity through an entry point. It is important to understand that chain analysis is not an exact science. It relies on heuristics derived from earlier observations or logical interpretations. These rules make it possible to obtain fairly reliable results, but never absolute precision. In other words, chain analysis always involves an element of plausibility in the conclusions reached. For example, one can estimate with more or less certainty that two addresses belong to the same entity, but total certainty will always be out of reach. The whole objective of chain analysis lies precisely in aggregating various heuristics in order to minimize the risk of error. It is, in a way, an accumulation of evidence that lets us come closer to reality. These famous heuristics can be grouped into different categories: 
* Transaction *patterns* (or transaction models);
* Heuristics internal to the transaction;
* Heuristics external to the transaction. 

Note that the first two heuristics on Bitcoin were formulated by Satoshi Nakamoto himself. He sets them out in section 10 of the *White Paper*. It is interesting to observe that these two heuristics still hold a preeminent place in chain analysis today. They are the CIOH (*Common Input Ownership Heuristic*) and address reuse.

b9d5ad29-532b-4219-9f1c-a3e48703caa3.body:
Refers to the process of generating child key pairs from a parent key pair (private key and public key) and a chain code within a deterministic and hierarchical (HD) wallet. This process makes it possible to segment branches and organize a wallet into different levels with many child key pairs, all of which can be recovered knowing only the basic recovery information (the mnemonic phrase and the optional passphrase). To derive a child key, the `HMAC-SHA512` function is used with the parent chain code and the concatenation of the parent key and a 32-bit index. There are two types of derivation:
* Normal derivation, which uses the parent public key as the basis of the `HMAC-SHA512` function;
* Hardened derivation, which uses the parent private key as the basis of the `HMAC-SHA512` function;

The result of HMAC-SHA512 is split in two: the first 256 bits are added to the parent key to produce the child key (private, or public after multiplication by the generator point on the elliptic curve), and the remaining 256 bits become the child chain code.

c1574db6-f193-4f77-beb4-cbb3c54b28eb.body:
In the context of hierarchical deterministic (HD) wallets, the coin type (*coin type* in English) is a derivation level that makes it possible to differentiate the wallet's branches according to the different cryptocurrencies used. This derivation level, defined by BIP-0044, is located at depth 2 of the derivation structure, after the master key and the purpose (*purpose*). For example, for Bitcoin, the assigned index is `0x80000000`, noted `/0'/` in the derivation path. This means that all addresses and accounts derived from this path are associated with Bitcoin. This derivation level makes it possible to properly separate the different assets in a multi-currency wallet. Here are the indexes used for different cryptocurrencies:
* Bitcoin: `0x80000000`;
* Bitcoin Testnet: `0x80000001`;
* Litecoin: `0x80000002`;
* Dogecoin: `0x80000003`;
* Ethereum: `0x8000003c`...

![BIP-84 tree with an arrow pointing to the Coin Type level (m/84'/0'), which identifies the targeted cryptocurrency.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/t/type-de-devise/assets/image-1.png)

c84e350d-9afb-4716-b0c5-800f42070fb3.body:
Protocol designed by Tadge Dryja to compact the UTXO set of Bitcoin nodes using an accumulator built on Merkle trees. Unlike the classic UTXO set, which requires significant storage space, Utreexo drastically reduces the required memory by storing only the roots of the Merkle trees. This allows the node to verify the existence of the UTXOs used as transaction inputs, without having to keep the complete set of UTXOs. By using Utreexo, each node keeps only cryptographic fingerprints called Merkle roots. When a transaction is made, the user provides the inclusion proofs of the UTXOs and the corresponding Merkle paths. The node can thus verify transactions without storing the entire UTXO set. Let's take an example with a diagram to understand this mechanism:

![Utreexo inclusion proof: with UTXO 3, HASH 4 and HASH 1-2, the node reconstructs the Merkle root that it stores in RAM.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/u/utreexo/assets/image-1.png)

In this example, I intentionally reduced the UTXO set to 4 UTXOs to make it easier to understand. In reality, you have to imagine that there are almost 140 million UTXOs on Bitcoin as I write these lines. In this diagram, the Utreexo node would only have to keep the Merkle root in RAM. If it receives a transaction spending UTXO no. 3 (in black), the proof would consist of the following elements:
* UTXO 3;
* HASH 4;
* HASH 1-2.

Thanks to this information transmitted by the sender of the transaction, the Utreexo node performs the following verifications:
* It calculates the hash of UTXO 3, which gives it HASH 3;
* It concatenates HASH 3 with HASH 4;
* It calculates their hash, which gives it HASH 3-4;
* It concatenates HASH 3-4 with HASH 1-2;
* It calculates their hash, which gives it the Merkle root.

If the Merkle root it obtains through its process is the same as the Merkle root it stored in its RAM, then it is convinced that UTXO no. 3 is indeed part of the UTXO set.

This method reduces RAM requirements for full node operators. However, Utreexo has limitations, notably the increase in block size due to the additional proofs and the potential dependence of Utreexo nodes on *Bridge Nodes* to obtain the missing proofs. *Bridge Nodes* are traditional full nodes that provide the necessary proofs to Utreexo nodes, thus allowing complete verification. This approach offers a compromise between efficiency and decentralization, making transaction validation more accessible to users with limited resources.

c9bbca4b-b417-4c78-82db-62b9ffff043b.body:
Improvement proposal that introduces a standard hierarchical derivation structure for HD wallets. BIP-0044 builds on the principles established by BIP-0032 for key derivation and on BIP-0043 for the use of the “*purpose*” field. It introduces a structure of five derivation levels: `m / purpose' / coin_type' / account' / change / address_index`. Here is the detail of each depth:
* `m /` indicates the master private key. It is unique to a wallet and cannot have siblings at the same depth. The master key is derived directly from the wallet's seed;
* `m / purpose' /` indicates the derivation purpose, which makes it possible to identify the standard followed. This field is described in BIP-0043. For example, if the wallet follows the BIP-0084 standard (SegWit V0), the index will then be `84'`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' /` indicates the type of cryptocurrency. This makes it possible to clearly distinguish the branches dedicated to one cryptocurrency from the branches dedicated to another cryptocurrency on a multi-coin wallet. The index dedicated to Bitcoin is `0'`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' /` indicates the account number. This depth makes it possible to easily differentiate and organize a wallet into different accounts. These accounts are numbered starting from `0'`. The extended keys (`xpub`, `xprv`...) are found at this depth level;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' / change /` indicates the chain. Each account as defined at depth 3 has two chains at depth 4: an external chain and an internal chain (also called “*change*”). The external chain derives addresses intended to be shared publicly, that is, the addresses offered to us when we click on “receive” in our wallet software. The internal chain derives addresses intended not to be shared publicly, that is, mainly change addresses. The external chain is identified with index `0` and the internal chain is identified with index `1`. You will notice that from this depth onward, we no longer perform a hardened derivation, but a normal derivation (there is no apostrophe). It is thanks to this mechanism that we are able to derive all the child public keys from their `xpub`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' / change / address-index` simply indicates the number of the receiving address and its key pair, in order to differentiate it from its siblings at the same depth on the same branch. For example, the first derived address has index `0`, the second address has index `1`, etc...

For example, if my receiving address has the derivation path `m / 86' / 0' / 0' / 0 / 5`, we can deduce the following information:
* `86'` indicates that we are following the BIP-0086 derivation standard (Taproot or SegWit V1);
* `0'` indicates that it is a Bitcoin address;
* `0'` indicates that we are on the first account of the wallet;
* `0` indicates that it is an external address;
* `5` indicates that it is the sixth external address of this account.

![BIP-84 tree illustrating the five-level BIP-44 structure: purpose, coin type, accounts, chains and addresses.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/b/bip-0044/assets/image-1.png)

c9cf3f68-6ddc-49f2-998d-0b11219e5b5b.body:
Concatenates the two items at the top of the stack (that is, puts them end to end). This opcode has been disabled, so it is currently impossible to use. However, it has recently come back into the spotlight. Some would like to add it to Tapscript to allow items on the stack to be combined, and thus improve the expressiveness of this language. This simple additional tool could allow:
* The use of Lamport signatures, which are believed to be resistant to quantum attacks;
* The implementation of vaults;
* The use of covenants;
* Or the use of non-equivocation contracts.

ca7ad011-0bf8-4b20-ad10-a94cf422d118.body:
Establishes a new standardized process for the classification of Bitcoin improvement proposals. BIPs must from now on be classified according to 4 layers (*layers*):
* `Consensus`: concerns proposals that require a consensus change and affect compatibility between earlier and future versions of the Bitcoin protocol. These are soft forks and hard forks;
* `Peer Services`: concerns changes to the services and communication protocols between network nodes, without affecting consensus;
* `API/RPC`: encompasses proposals aiming to modify the APIs and RPCs used to interact with Bitcoin nodes;
* `Applications`: includes proposals for improvements to applications that run on top of the Bitcoin network, such as typically the standards related to wallet software.

d39ccdd1-24d3-4516-a077-bde3508aa1c7.body:
Parameter in a Bitcoin transaction that determines which components of a transaction (inputs and outputs) are covered by the associated signature, which thereby become immutable. The SigHash Flag is a byte added to the digital signature of each input. The choice of SigHash Flag therefore directly affects which parts of the transaction are frozen by the signature and which can still be modified afterward. This mechanism ensures that signatures commit to the transaction data precisely and securely, according to the signer's intent. Three main SigHash Flags exist:

* `SIGHASH_ALL` (`0x01`): The signature applies to all of the transaction's inputs and outputs, thereby locking them completely;

* `SIGHASH_NONE` (`0x02`): The signature applies to all inputs, but to no outputs, allowing the outputs to be modified after signing;

* `SIGHASH_SINGLE` (`0x03`): The signature covers all inputs and only one output, the one corresponding to the index of the signed input.

In addition to these three SigHash Flags, the modifier `SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY` (`0x80`) can be combined with each of the preceding types. When this modifier is used, only one input is signed, leaving the others open to modification. Here are the existing combinations with the modifier:

* `SIGHASH_ALL | SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY` (`0x81`): The signature applies to a single input while covering all of the transaction's outputs;

* `SIGHASH_NONE | SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY` (`0x82`): The signature covers a single input, without committing to any output;

* `SIGHASH_SINGLE | SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY` (`0x83`): The signature applies to a single input and only to the output with the same index as that input.

*A synonym sometimes used for “SigHash” is “Signature Hash Types”.*

d4857dab-1e59-424a-8e4d-8053d34b85ae.body:
In the context of RGB, the *Contract State* corresponds to the current state of a contract at a given moment. It can consist of both public and private data, which reflect the situation of the contract. In RGB, a distinction is made between:
* The *Global State*, which comprises the public properties of the contract (set up from the *Genesis* or added via authorized updates);
* The *Owned States*, which belong to specific holders, identified by their UTXOs.

dd7fef86-8eb0-4ed5-ba65-899c7374b274.body:
Data structure in the Bitcoin system. A block contains a set of valid transactions and metadata contained in its header. Each block is linked to the next by the hash of its header, thus forming the blockchain (chain of blocks). The blockchain acts as a timestamp server that allows each user to know all past transactions, in order to verify the non-existence of a transaction and prevent double spending. Transactions are organized in a Merkle tree. This cryptographic accumulator makes it possible to produce a digest of all the transactions in a block, called the “Merkle root” (*Merkle root*). The header of a block contains 6 elements:
* The block version;
* The hash of the previous block;
* The root of the transactions' Merkle tree;
* The block timestamp;
* The difficulty target;
* The nonce.

To be valid, a block must have a header that, once hashed with `SHA256d`, produces a digest less than or equal to the difficulty target.

e48225f8-71a1-4a02-8713-0e15827026f2.body:
Data structure provided in the witness of a P2TR transaction during a script path spend (*script path spend*), as defined in BIP-0341. The control block proves that a given script is indeed part of the script tree (MAST) that was used to build the output's Taproot public key.

It consists of three elements:
* a first byte that encodes the leaf script version (*leaf version*) and the parity of the output public key;
* the 32-byte internal public key (*internal key*), which is the key before the tweak is applied;
* the Merkle path, made up of zero to 128 hashes of 32 bytes each, which represents the sequence of sibling nodes needed to recompute the root of the tree from the revealed leaf.

During validation, the node recomputes the Merkle root from the leaf script and the provided path, then checks that the tweak of the internal public key with this root matches the output public key recorded in the `scriptPubKey`. This mechanism makes it possible to prove that a script belongs to the tree without revealing the other leaves, which preserves the privacy of the unused spending paths.

ef70aa4a-7364-4b70-8d04-f628c67b1404.body:
In the general context of computing, a payload designates the essential data carried in a larger data packet. For example, in a SegWit V0 address on Bitcoin, the payload corresponds to the hash of the public key, without the various metadata (the HRP, the separator, the SegWit version, and the checksum). For example, in the address `bc1qc2eukw7reasfcmrafevp5dhv8635yuqays50gj`, we have: 
* `bc`: the human-readable part (HRP);
* `1`: the separator;
* `q`: the SegWit version. Here, it is version 0;
* `c2eukw7reasfcmrafevp5dhv8635yuqa`: the payload, here, the hash of the public key;
* `ys50gj`: the checksum.

f83f1367-fa30-4cbd-bc65-6cb6270f5f26.body:
The set of all the rules observed by the software of a Bitcoin node in its interactions with the peer-to-peer network. Relay policy is the most encompassing term for designating a node's local rules, as opposed to consensus rules, which are universal.

Relay policy breaks down into increasingly specific subsets:
* The *transaction relay policy* concerns specifically the rules related to the handling of transactions on the network;
* The *mempool policy* groups together the rules for accepting transactions into the node's mempool;
* The *standardness rules* dictate the format of the individual transactions accepted.

Unlike consensus rules, relay policy is configured locally by each node and may vary from one to another.

fc722a57-45f7-4263-b2a1-6d3bf7b8c060.body:
Information used to receive bitcoins. An address is generally built by hashing a public key, using `SHA256` and `RIPEMD160`, and adding metadata to this digest. The public keys used to build a receiving address are part of the user's wallet and are therefore derived from its seed. For example, SegWit addresses are composed of the following information: 
* An HRP to designate “bitcoin”: `bc`; 
* A separator: `1`; 
* The SegWit version used: `q` or `p`; 
* The payload: the digest of the public key (or directly the public key in the case of Taproot); 
* The checksum: a BCH code.

But a receiving address can also represent something else depending on the script template used. For example, P2SH addresses are built using the hash of the script. Taproot addresses, for their part, directly contain the tweaked public key (*tweaked*) without it being hashed.

A receiving address can be represented as a string of alphanumeric characters or as a QR code. Each address can be used several times, but this is a highly discouraged practice. Indeed, in order to maintain a certain level of privacy, it is advised to use each Bitcoin address only once. A new one must be generated for every incoming payment to one's wallet. An address is encoded in `Bech32` for SegWit V0 addresses, in `Bech32m` for SegWit V1 addresses, and in `Base58check` for Legacy addresses. From a technical point of view, receiving bitcoin translates into possessing the private key associated with a public key (and therefore with an address). When someone receives bitcoins, the sender updates the existing constraint on their spending so that only the recipient can now have this power.

